### Estimation Methodology

**SEC HIIP Claim:** The SEC HIIP estimates are raw, un-risked baselines. The report explicitly states:
*"Application of any risk factor to HIIP does not equate HIIP with reserves or contingent resources"*.
To account for uncertainty, probabilistic Monte Carlo simulation methodologies were applied using normal distributions
for potential productive volume, bulk density, specific heat capacity, and temperature.

**HIP-RA-X Evaluation:**
By default, HIP-RA-X acts as a resource assessment tool and bakes in a 75% rock heat recovery factor.
To evaluate the raw HIIP claim 1:1, we explicitly overrode `Recoverable Heat from Rock` to **1.0 (100%)**.

Furthermore, to mirror the SEC filing's probabilistic approach, GEOPHIRES's [Monte Carlo module](Monte-Carlo-User-Guide.html)
was utilized, supplying normal distributions for Reservoir Temperature, Reservoir Area, Reservoir Thickness,
Rock Heat Capacity, and Density of Reservoir Rock. The central temperature is derived from the filing's
per-interval figures rather than taken as the midpoint of the quoted range; see
[Thermal structure of the accumulation](#thermal-structure-of-the-accumulation) below.

## Results

### Estimation of Heat Initially in Place

**SEC HIIP Claim:** The probabilistic evaluation yielded a "Low Estimate" (P90) Gross HIIP of 50,730 PJ and a
"Mean Estimate" of 63,560 PJ.

**HIP-RA-X Evaluation:**
Our evaluation captures both the deterministic proxy (using the 199°C ORC intake temperature) and the probabilistic
Mean (using the 1,000-iteration Monte Carlo simulation).

| Model | Evaluated Thermal Metric | Result (10<sup>15</sup> Joules) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **SEC Filing (D&M)** | Gross HIIP (Low Estimate) | **50,730** |
| **HIP-RA-X (Deterministic)** | Stored Heat (reservoir) | **{{ det_stored_heat_15j }}** |
| **SEC Filing (D&M)** | Gross HIIP (Mean Estimate) | **63,560** |
| **HIP-RA-X (Monte Carlo)**| Stored Heat (reservoir) Mean | **{{ mc_stored_heat_mean_15j }}** |

Because our deterministic run used a static 199°C input, sitting in the lower half of the SEC's 170°C to 250°C
range, it maps closely to the P90 "Low Estimate", agreeing to within 0.5%.

#### Thermal structure of the accumulation

The central temperature for the probabilistic run requires more care than the deterministic case. The
midpoint of the 170°C to 250°C range is 210°C, but the accumulation is not thermally uniform, and a
Monte Carlo centred on 210°C returns a mean Stored Heat approximately 13% below the reported mean HIIP.

D&M's own figures show the structure. Their mean capacity per unit rock volume rises with depth, from
66 MW/km³ in the Base interval to 77 and 78 MW/km³ in Benches 1 and 2. Taking each interval's volume as
its mean capacity divided by its capacity per unit rock volume, and its temperature as the value that
reproduces its reported Gross HIIP at the rock heat capacity adopted above:

| Interval | Mean capacity (MW) | MW/km<sup>3</sup> | Volume (km<sup>3</sup>) | Gross HIIP (10<sup>15</sup> J) | Implied temperature |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Base (0–3,200 m) | 5,908 | 66 | 89.5 | 26,815 | 215°C |
| Bench 1 (3,200–3,600 m) | 4,018 | 77 | 52.2 | 18,234 | 238°C |
| Bench 2 (3,600–4,000 m) | 4,078 | 78 | 52.3 | 18,510 | 240°C |
| **Total** | **14,005** | **73** | **194.0** | **63,559** | **228°C** |

Three features support the reconstruction rather than merely fitting it. The interval HIIP values sum to
the reported mean of 63,560. The implied temperatures increase monotonically with depth although nothing
constrains them to. And all three fall inside the stated 170°C to 250°C window.

The volume-weighted mean of 228°C, rather than the 210°C range midpoint, is therefore the appropriate
central value for a lumped probabilistic run, and is what the Monte Carlo above uses.

Two limitations are worth stating plainly. These are effective temperatures that reproduce the reported
HIIP at the rock heat capacity adopted here; a different heat capacity would shift them. And because D&M
publish neither areal extent nor net thickness, the volumes carry no geometric interpretation — the
baseline's area and thickness are chosen so that their product reproduces the productive volume, not as
claims about the resource's shape.

